{"id":"log-transform-skewed-features","t":"Log transform for skewed features","tags":["Feature Engineering"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"log_transform","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"Heavy-tailed, non-negative features (prices, counts, durations) are often easier for models after a log transform. Write `log_transform(X, threshold)` for a 2-D float array `X` of shape `(n, d)`:\n\n1. For each column compute the sample skewness $g = \\frac{\\frac1n\\sum (x - \\bar x)^3}{\\left(\\frac1n \\sum (x-\\bar x)^2\\right)^{3/2}}$ (use 0 when the column is constant).\n2. A column is transformed if $g >$ `threshold` **and** its minimum is $\\ge 0$. Transformed columns become `np.log1p(x)`; others are unchanged.\n\nReturn a tuple `(X_new, transformed)` where `transformed` is a list of the transformed column indices in increasing order.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef log_transform(X, threshold):\n    \"\"\"Return (X_new, transformed_column_indices).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Skewness is the third central moment divided by the variance to the power 3/2. Compute it per column.","Use `np.log1p`, which is log(1 + x): it maps 0 to 0 and is accurate for small x.","Only transform columns with no negative values; log1p of values below -1 is undefined."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef log_transform(X, threshold):\n    X = np.asarray(X, dtype=float)\n    out, cols = X.copy(), []\n    for j in range(X.shape[1]):\n        x = X[:, j]\n        c = x - x.mean()\n        m2 = (c ** 2).mean()\n        g = 0.0 if m2 == 0 else (c ** 3).mean() / m2 ** 1.5\n        if g > threshold and x.min() >= 0:\n            out[:, j] = np.log1p(x)\n            cols.append(j)\n    return out, cols\n","ex_md":"## Why log helps\nA log turns multiplicative differences into additive ones: going from 10 to 100 and from 100 to 1,000 become equal steps. Linear models and distance-based methods then see a more even spread, and a few huge values no longer dominate squared-error losses. Tree models are invariant to monotonic transforms, so they gain little.\n\n## Why log1p\nCounts often include zeros, and $\\log 0$ is undefined. $\\log(1 + x)$ handles zeros and behaves like $x$ for small values. For data with negative values, alternatives are the Yeo-Johnson transform or a signed log, $\\mathrm{sign}(x)\\log(1 + |x|)$.\n\n## Fit on training data only\nWhich columns to transform is a decision learned from data; make it on the training split and apply the same list to validation and test.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- How do you report predictions in original units if the target itself was log-transformed? (Exponentiating the mean of a log prediction gives a median, not a mean.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[  1.,   5.],\n       [  2.,   5.],\n       [100.,   5.]])\n0.5","out":"(array([[0.69314718, 5.        ],\n       [1.09861229, 5.        ],\n       [4.61512052, 5.        ]]), [0])"},{"in":"array([[ 7.69768105e+00, -8.86459943e-01],\n       [ 7.76405815e-02, -2.91720232e-01],\n       [ 1.51907082e+00,  8.82538967e-01],\n       [ 5.66788191e-01,  5.80350016e-01],\n       [ 6.35941124e-01,  9.15167033e-02],\n       [ 8.06059947e-01,  6.70104355e-01],\n       [ 1.32657305e-01, -2.82816231e+00],\n       [ 7.92999746e-01,  1.02130682e+00],\n       [ 4.20961846e-01, -9.59644760e-01],\n       [ 2.77434428e+01, -1.66861984e+00],\n       [ 1.25330820e+00,  2.76445760e-01],\n       [ 7.02836637e-01,  7.00544885e-01],\n       [ 7.54811358e-01, -4.44767456e-01],\n       [ 5.12709256e-01, -1.07640584e+00] …\n1.0","out":"(array([[ 2.16305644, -0.88645994],\n       [ 0.074774  , -0.29172023],\n       [ 0.92389011,  0.88253897],\n       [ 0.44902779,  0.58035002],\n       [ 0.49221825,  0.0915167 ],\n       [ 0.59114765,  0.67010435],\n       [ 0.12456647, -2.82816231],\n       [ 0.58389005,  1.02130682],\n       [ 0.351334  , -0.95964476],\n       [ 3.35840966, -1.66861984],\n       [ 0.81239945,  0.27644576],\n       [ 0.53229547,  0.70054489],\n       [ 